---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import SignalFlow from '../../components/SignalFlow.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Signal Chain & Routing"
  description="Follow the Ardor signal from your guitar input to the stereo output."
  lede="Ardor shows the signal path from left to right. Place blocks in the order that you want them to process your sound."
>
  <h2>Follow the signal</h2>
  <p>
    Your guitar enters at <strong>IN</strong>. Ardor sends the signal through each block in
    order, then sends it to <strong>OUT</strong>. The safety limiter stays at the end.
  </p>
  <SignalFlow />

  <h2>Build a sound from left to right</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Start with the input.</li>
    <li>Add drive, amp, and cabinet blocks.</li>
    <li>Add modulation, delay, or reverb after the cabinet.</li>
    <li>End at the output.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Drag a block to change its place. Tap <strong>+</strong> to add a block. The editor keeps
    the path horizontal, so you can see what happens before and after each block.
  </p>

  <h2>Use two paths</h2>
  <p>
    Add a Dual Rig when one path is not enough. The guitar signal enters the split, then runs
    through two horizontal paths. The left path feeds the left output. The right path feeds
    the right output. The paths join as a stereo signal.
  </p>
  <p>
    See <a href={url('/docs/dual-rig')}>Dual Rig &amp; Dual Amp</a> for the two-path layout.
  </p>

  <h2>Keep the level clear</h2>
  <p>
    Use the input level to change how hard you drive the amp model. Use the output level to
    change the final level. If the sound overloads, reduce the control that comes before the
    overload.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it changes</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Input gain</td><td>The level that enters the amp model.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Output gain</td><td>The level after the cabinet.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Safety limit</td><td>A protective ceiling at the end of the chain.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      If the amp sound loses its character, lower the input gain. If the amp sound is right
      but the output clips, lower the output gain. The clip meter helps you find the first
      overload.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Choose the right effect order</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Put the amp model before its cabinet sound.</li>
    <li>Put the cabinet before stereo delay and reverb.</li>
    <li>Put modulation where you want the movement in the sound.</li>
    <li>Use Dual Rig when you need separate left and right amp paths.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Some blocks can accept either mono or stereo sound. Ardor applies the correct conversion
    when you use a block in a valid place.
  </p>

  <p>
    See the{' '}<a href={url('/docs/effects')}>Effects Reference</a> for every block, or use the{' '}
    <a href={url('/docs/manager')}>Manager App</a> to edit a long chain on your computer.
  </p>

  <details>
    <summary>Advanced: mono and stereo rules</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>AMP and cabinet stages use a mono signal. An amp stage can fold a stereo input to mono.</li>
      <li>A cabinet should come before stereo effects.</li>
      <li>Modulation keeps the stereo field that it receives.</li>
      <li>Delay and reverb keep the dry stereo signal and create their own stereo wet signal.</li>
      <li>Dual Amp and Dual Rig create stereo with separate left and right paths.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Input mode</h3>
    <p>An amp model can choose how it folds a stereo input into mono:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Input used</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="mono">sum</td><td>The average of left and right. This is the default.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">left</td><td>The left channel.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">right</td><td>The right channel.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Advanced: preset field names</summary>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Location</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="mono">inputGainDb</td><td>Before the amp model</td><td>Set the drive into the model.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">outputGainDb</td><td>After the cabinet</td><td>Set the output level.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">safetyLimitDb</td><td>End of chain</td><td>Set the protective ceiling. The default is −1 dBFS.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </details>
</DocsLayout>
